<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bocas del Toro Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bocas del Toro is a province in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> and including the Bocas del Toro archipelago. From the late nineteenth century it became a centre of banana growing, dominated from 1899 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a>, which brought workers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and other Caribbean islands. Its population includes many Ngäbe people and descendants of Afro-Caribbean migrants.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bocas-del-toro-province.jpg" alt="Bocas del Toro Province" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Panapro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bocas2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bocas del Toro, meaning Mouths of the Bull, has an area of 4,643.9 square kilometres, comprising the mainland and nine main islands. The province consists of the Bocas del Toro Archipelago, Almirante Bay, Chiriquí Lagoon and the adjacent mainland. It borders the Caribbean Sea to the north, Limón Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> and the Naso Tjër Di Comarca to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiriqui-province">Chiriquí Province</a> to the south and the Ngöbe-Buglé Comarca to the east, and the Río Sixaola forms part of the border with Costa Rica. The capital is the city of Bocas del Toro, or Bocas Town, on Colón Island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> explored the area in 1502 while searching for a passage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>. His original name for the island was Isla del Drago. In colonial times the area was part of Veraguas. It was originally part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> until, in one of many territorial disputes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> took control of it with armed forces, and in 1834 the government made a reservation called Bocas del Toro. In 1850 it became a part of Chiriquí and was then separated from it to become part of Colón. On 16 November 1903 Bocas del Toro was separated from Colón and became its own province.</p><p>In 1941 the province was divided into two districts, Bocas del Toro and Crimamola, and four years later it returned to its earlier division. In 1970 the district of Bocas del Toro became Changuinola, the district of Bastimentos was eliminated and three new districts were added. The districts' areas changed in 1997, when the Ngöbe-Buglé Comarca was created, and again in 2020, when the Naso Tjër Di Comarca was established. The province is the home of the Bocas del Toro Creole of English.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The province has two national parks: Isla Bastimentos National Marine Park, and La Amistad International Park, which spans the Costa Rica-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> border and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The population was 159,228 in 2023. It is divided into four districts and 40 corregimientos, and its other main towns are Almirante and Changuinola. The Smithsonian Tropical Research Institute runs a research station on Colón Island, and banana plantations in the province are often called the oro verde, or green gold, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bocas del Toro Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The interior provinces lived from cattle ranching, rice and subsistence farming, while the United Fruit Company grew bananas in <b>Bocas del Toro</b> and Chiriquí.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
